 {
  "node_id": "p24_27",
  "index": 27,
  "text": "Figure3.1 The possible communication architectures for multiplayer and network games:\na)single screen, possibly ‘split’, on a single computer, b)peer-to-peer, c)a client–server,\nd)peer-to-peer-clientserverhybrid,ande)networkofservers",
  "bbox": [
   46.08,
   277.38980000000004,
   416.7366255,
   308.6764225
  ],
  "font": "GFOBMP+Times-Roman",
  "font_size": 9.22,
  "line_count": 3,
  "translation_status": "preserved"
 },
 {
  "node_id": "p24_28",
  "index": 28,
  "text": "AreaNetworks(WANs)(i.e.theInternet),theydonotscalewellwithoutanadditional\nhierarchicalstructure.\nInaclient–serverarchitecture,oneprocessplaystheroleoftheserver,communicating\nwitheachclientandmediatingthegamestate.Theclientsdonotcommunicatedirectly\nwith each other but rather have the server route messages to the appropriate clients.\nTheserveristhecriticalpartinthecommunicationlink;ifaclientcannotcommunicate\nwiththeserverthegamecannotbeplayedandiftheservercannotkeepupwiththe\ncommunicationandcomputationrequired,thegameplaycandegradeforallclients.The\nclient–server architecture is the most popular architecture used in commercial online\ngamesaswellasintheclassicMultiUserDungeon(MUD)games(seeChapter2fora\ndescriptionofaMUD).\nInthepeer-to-peer,client–serverhybridarchitecture,theserverprocessmediatesgame\nstatesonthebasisofinformationsentbyclientsasinthetraditionalclient–serverarchi-\ntecture,buttheclientsarealsoabletocommunicatewithotherclientsasinthetraditional\npeer-to-peerarchitecture.Thecommunicationamongsttheclientpeersisgenerallyfor\ngameinformationthatisnotessentialforachievingconsistentviewsofthegamestate",
